<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julio Argentino Roca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1843">1843</a>–<a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julio Argentino Roca was an Argentine general and statesman, twice president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, from 1880 to 1886 and from 1898 to 1904. As minister of war he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conquest-of-the-desert">Conquest of the Desert</a> of 1878–1879, which destroyed the independence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranquel">Ranquel</a> and other peoples of the pampas and opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patagonia">Patagonia</a> to settlement. His presidencies consolidated the long dominance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-autonomist-party">National Autonomist Party</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julio-argentino-roca.jpg" alt="Julio Argentino Roca" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Julio_roca_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Julio Argentino Roca was born on 17 July 1843 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-miguel-de-tucuman">San Miguel de Tucumán</a> into a prominent local family and studied at the National College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concepcion-del-uruguay">Concepción del Uruguay</a>. Before he was fifteen he joined the army of the Argentine Confederation and fought in the struggle between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> and the interior provinces. He fought in the War of the Triple Alliance against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> from 1865, in which his father and two of his brothers died. Later he rose to colonel in the war against the revolt of Ricardo López Jordán, and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-avellaneda">Nicolás Avellaneda</a> promoted him to general after his victory at Santa Rosa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1878 Roca became minister of war under Avellaneda. Frontier groups defended their traditional territories and raided settlements. His predecessor Adolfo Alsina had ordered a ditch and a line of small forts across Buenos Aires Province, but Roca saw no way to end the raids except by bringing all land up to the Río Negro under government control, in a campaign that would extinguish, subdue or expel the Indians living there. This <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conquest-of-the-desert">Conquest of the Desert</a> began against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranquel">Ranqueles</a> in 1878–1879. Roca commanded it in the field, and it is sometimes considered a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>.</p><p>The campaigns went on after 1880 in Neuquén and Río Negro, in the Andes and in a final phase to 1885, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> completed the occupation of what are now Neuquén, Chubut and southern Río Negro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Elected in 1880 as the candidate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-autonomist-party">National Autonomist Party</a>, Roca was inaugurated amid a revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> that cost 3,000 lives and ended on 25 June with an agreement. Congress federalised the city of Buenos Aires. His first term (1880–1886) saw large public works, secular reforms including a civil registry and Law 1420 on compulsory, free and secular primary education. A treaty of 1881 fixed the boundary with Chile along the Andes to the 52nd parallel.</p><p>He returned to the presidency on 12 October 1898. His second term saw the founding in 1901 of the first Argentine trade union federation and a first general strike in 1902, to which he responded with the Residence Law, which allowed the expulsion of immigrants without trial. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> on 19 October 1914 and is buried in La Recoleta Cemetery. Some currents of opinion criticise him as genocidal, and his name has been removed from several streets and schools.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Julio Argentino Roca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…home the army put down provincial uprisings and then, in the Conquest of the Desert led by General <b>Julio Argentino Roca</b> in 1878–1879 and continued into the mid-1880s, used Remington rifles, the telegraph and railways to overrun the lands of the Mapuche, Ranquel and Tehuelche peoples, killing and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Once hostility to Spain had faded and many Spanish immigrants had settled in the country, President <b>Julio Argentino Roca</b> decreed on 30 March 1900 that at official and public festivals and in schools only the first and last verses and the chorus should be sung, so no anti-Spanish…</blockquote><a href="/songs/argentine-national-anthem">Argentine National Anthem</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Manuel Namuncurá led the confederation until <b>Julio Argentino Roca's</b> Conquest of the Desert from 1878 to 1879 destroyed it.</blockquote><a href="/state/salinas-grandes-confederation">Salinas Grandes Confederation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ingenio La Trinidad, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-27.4,-65.52&amp;year=1889" aria-label="Ingenio La Trinidad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2828 3168 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Julio Argentino Roca"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3278 3438h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3278 3438h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-27.4,-65.52&amp;year=1889">What was here in 1889? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
